  "knowledgePattern": "Turn the principal square root into cases and intersect the domain",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Retain the principal root",
      "body": "<p>Replace the square root of a square by the corresponding modulus, then split at the input where the inner linear expression changes sign.</p>",
      "recap": "A principal square root is nonnegative, so the root of a square equals an absolute value.",
      "pitfall": "Dropping the modulus can reverse the left-hand expression on one side of its corner.",
      "pause": "Write the correct inequality in each branch.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-function-mapping",
          "reminder": "A principal square root is nonnegative, so the root of a square equals an absolute value."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Keep each branch’s restrictions",
      "body": "<p>Solve the polynomial inequality in a branch, then intersect its answer with that branch and the stated input interval.</p>",
      "recap": "An interval solution must satisfy every original condition, including branch restrictions.",
      "pitfall": "A quadratic root from one formula may lie outside the region where that formula applies.",
      "pause": "Identify all surviving intervals and add their lengths.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l04",
          "reminder": "An interval solution must satisfy every original condition, including branch restrictions."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The principal root is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, the inequality becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Its roots are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>. On this branch and inside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the surviving interval is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>. The root <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow></math> is outside the branch <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, the inequality is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Its roots are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>; this branch needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, so contributes nothing.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> The total length is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>, giving A. Open or closed endpoints do not change an interval’s length, but they matter when identifying the actual solution set.</p>"
